## Why This Is Asked
Testing offline-first sync across devices is a realistic challenge for consumer apps with data integrity concerns, multi-device usage, and eventual consistency.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first architecture: Data is persisted locally and synced when connectivity is restored
- Conflict resolution strategy: Merge (combine changes) vs. Last-Write-Wins (most recent timestamp wins)
- Test isolation across devices: Each Playwright context maintains separate local storage and cookies
- Deterministic network shaping: Use Playwright's `route` or Cypress's `server` to intercept and delay requests

## Code Example
```javascript
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('offline-first sync with conflict resolution', async ({ browser }) => {
  // Create two isolated device contexts
  const ctxA = await browser.newContext();
  const ctxB = await browser.newContext();

  const pageA = await ctxA.newPage();
  const pageB = await ctxB.newPage();

  // Simulate offline on both devices
  await pageA.route('**/api/**', route => route.abort('failed'));
  await pageB.route('**/api/**', route => route.abort('failed'));

  // Device A creates a note offline
  await pageA.goto('/app');
  await pageA.fill('[data-testid="note-input"]', 'Note from A');
  await pageA.click('[data-testid="save"]');

  // Device B creates a note offline
  await pageB.goto('/app');
  await pageB.fill('[data-testid="note-input"]', 'Note from B');
  await pageB.click('[data-testid="save"]');

  // Restore connectivity
  await pageA.unrouteAll();
  await pageB.unrouteAll();

  // Wait for sync and verify
  await pageA.reload();
  await pageB.reload();

  await expect(pageA.locator('[data-testid="notes"]')).toContainText('Note from A');
  await expect(pageA.locator('[data-testid="notes"]')).toContainText('Note from B');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
